// Digital twin: Asset Administration Shell 3.2 and AutomationML 2.1: the standards' constraints, their sample data and the links between them
//
// Generated by ../../crosswalk.py from crosswalk.json, sample.json and the model.json and sample.json of each
// standard. Edit those and regenerate, rather than this file.
//
// Each standard's labels and relationship types are prefixed: Asset Administration Shell 3.2 as aas__, AutomationML
// Edition 2.1 (CAEX 3.0) as aml__. The links between them are not.
//
// The belt conveyor of assembly cell AC-1 is driven by the Halden HSM 140 servo motor, serial number 2407-00381,
// whose asset administration shell Halden Antriebe publishes. Kellerman Automation adds the drive to the conveyor in
// its AutomationML file, playing both the actuating drive role and the AAS asset role, with its serial number as an
// attribute whose RefSemantic is the ECLASS IRDI of the nameplate's serial number. Inside the drive it carries the
// motor's shell as Application Recommendation AR 004E serialises it: an internal element with the
// AssetAdministrationShell role, named by the shell's idShort and holding the shell's id, with the Nameplate and
// TechnicalData submodels and, inside the technical data, the TechnicalProperties collection and its RatedTorque
// property, whose value and semanticId are attributes. Links join the AAS sample's shell, asset information,
// submodels, collection and property to those elements; the nameplate's serial number property to the drive's
// attribute it publishes, and its semantic ID to the attribute's RefSemantic; and the rated torque's semanticId
// reference to the attribute serialising it. Asset Administration Shell 3.2: The digital twin of a synchronous servo
// motor, serial number 2407-00381, from Halden Antriebe GmbH, a fictional drive maker: an asset administration shell
// with its asset information and specific asset IDs, and two submodels following the IDTA templates, Digital
// Nameplate and Technical Data. Their elements, among them multi-language properties, a list of markings, collections
// and a range, carry semantic IDs pointing at ECLASS IRDIs, the templates' IRIs or the maker's concept descriptions,
// which define rated torque and degree of protection by IEC 61360 data specifications and the newton metre by a unit
// of measure data specification. AutomationML Edition 2.1 (CAEX 3.0): A CAEX file describing assembly cell AC-1 of a
// fictional plant builder, exchanged between its plant engineering and control engineering tools: an instance
// hierarchy of a work cell holding a welding robot with a vacuum gripper, a belt conveyor and a PLC, their ports and
// signal interfaces, the internal links between them, and attributes with units, semantics and nominal constraints.
// Behind the instances stand a system unit class library with the robot and gripper types — the gripper's supported
// role class mapped to a user-defined role class by attribute name — and user-defined role and interface class
// libraries, alongside external references to AutomationML's standard libraries.
//
// Run it against an empty database of Neo4j 5 or later, Enterprise Edition, for its node key and property existence
// constraints, e.g. with cypher-shell -f model.cypher. It merges every node and relationship, so it can be run again.

// The standards: their constraints

